1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:04,359
The prisoners had the keys. The guards were hostages. For eleven days,

2
00:00:04,409 --> 00:00:07,214
the government negotiated to get its prison back.

3
00:00:08,564 --> 00:00:13,189
Easter Sunday, nineteen ninety three. A maximum security prison in Ohio.

4
00:00:13,239 --> 00:00:17,020
Around four hundred inmates take over an entire cell block,

5
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:18,838
with twelve officers inside.

6
00:00:19,588 --> 00:00:21,622
Before it ends, ten men will be dead.

7
00:00:21,672 --> 00:00:23,538
A message will hang from a window.

8
00:00:23,588 --> 00:00:26,017
A guard's body will be thrown into the yard.

9
00:00:26,067 --> 00:00:29,736
And the prisoners will make the state put them on live television.

10
00:00:30,486 --> 00:00:35,489
It became one of the longest and deadliest prison sieges in modern American history.

11
00:00:35,539 --> 00:00:37,414
And it started with a skin test.

12
00:00:38,384 --> 00:00:40,286
Chapter 1. The powder keg.

13
00:00:42,416 --> 00:00:47,683
The Southern Ohio Correctional Facility sits in the hills near the small town of Lucasville,

14
00:00:47,733 --> 00:00:51,151
in the far south of Ohio. It opened in nineteen seventy two.

15
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:54,792
It was built to hold the most dangerous prisoners in the state.

16
00:00:55,542 --> 00:00:59,780
Ohio already knew what a prison disaster looked like. In nineteen thirty,

17
00:00:59,830 --> 00:01:05,185
a fire at the old Ohio Penitentiary in Columbus killed three hundred and twenty two inmates.

18
00:01:05,235 --> 00:01:06,712
For more than sixty years,

19
00:01:06,762 --> 00:01:10,413
that was the darkest day in the history of the state's prisons.

20
00:01:11,163 --> 00:01:15,584
Lucasville was meant to be the modern answer. Concrete, steel, long corridors,

21
00:01:15,634 --> 00:01:18,908
and cell blocks that could be sealed off from one another.

22
00:01:18,958 --> 00:01:22,633
On paper, a riot there should have been contained within minutes.

23
00:01:23,383 --> 00:01:26,095
By nineteen ninety three, it was dangerously full.

24
00:01:26,145 --> 00:01:27,863
According to Ohio's own figures,

25
00:01:27,913 --> 00:01:33,829
the state's prisons were running at more than one hundred and seventy eight percent of their rated capacity.

26
00:01:33,879 --> 00:01:40,402
A former head of Ohio's prison system later said Lucasville held probably double what a maximum security prison should.

27
00:01:41,152 --> 00:01:43,206
Cells built for one man held two.

28
00:01:43,256 --> 00:01:46,649
Violent prisoners from rival gangs lived side by side.

29
00:01:46,699 --> 00:01:50,219
And a new warden, Arthur Tate, was tightening the rules.

30
00:01:50,969 --> 00:01:54,848
Ohio's prison population had exploded in the nineteen eighties.

31
00:01:54,898 --> 00:01:56,782
In early nineteen ninety three,

32
00:01:56,832 --> 00:01:59,900
the state held more than forty thousand prisoners,

33
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:03,580
guarded by a system of around ten thousand employees. Work,

34
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:06,137
education and programs could not keep up.

35
00:02:06,187 --> 00:02:09,255
Thousands of men had almost nothing to do all day.

36
00:02:10,005 --> 00:02:14,376
One former reporter who covered the prison described it simply. Lucasville,

37
00:02:14,426 --> 00:02:16,203
he said, was like a powder keg.

38
00:02:16,953 --> 00:02:18,667
The spark was tuberculosis.

39
00:02:20,217 --> 00:02:25,303
In the early nineteen nineties, tuberculosis was spreading in crowded American prisons.

40
00:02:25,353 --> 00:02:28,078
The warden ordered every prisoner to be tested.

41
00:02:28,128 --> 00:02:30,793
The test was a small injection under the skin.

42
00:02:31,543 --> 00:02:33,808
But the testing fluid contained phenol,

43
00:02:33,858 --> 00:02:39,030
a substance that some Muslim prisoners said they could not accept for religious reasons.

44
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:41,701
They asked for another method. A chest x ray,

45
00:02:41,751 --> 00:02:45,440
or a sputum sample, which had been used at another Ohio prison.

46
00:02:46,190 --> 00:02:51,102
The warden refused. The testing would go ahead, starting Monday, April twelfth.

47
00:02:51,152 --> 00:02:55,687
The Muslim prisoners decided to protest the day before. On Easter Sunday.

48
00:02:56,657 --> 00:02:58,605
Chapter 2. Easter Sunday.

49
00:03:00,735 --> 00:03:03,979
April eleventh, nineteen ninety three. It was a holiday,

50
00:03:04,029 --> 00:03:08,037
and staffing was reduced. The warden was away for the Easter weekend.

51
00:03:08,787 --> 00:03:09,847
In the afternoon,

52
00:03:09,897 --> 00:03:14,089
prisoners were coming back into L block from the recreation yard.

53
00:03:14,139 --> 00:03:18,135
L block was a big complex. Eight cell blocks, and a gymnasium,

54
00:03:18,185 --> 00:03:19,897
all connected by corridors.

55
00:03:20,647 --> 00:03:24,251
At around three in the afternoon, according to the official report,

56
00:03:24,301 --> 00:03:27,360
a fight broke out. Officers moved in. And within minutes,

57
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:29,760
it was clear this was not an ordinary fight.

58
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:33,699
According to later accounts, the original plan was limited.

59
00:03:33,749 --> 00:03:36,005
Take over part of the block, barricade it,

60
00:03:36,055 --> 00:03:40,122
and demand that someone from the state prison headquarters come and listen.

61
00:03:40,172 --> 00:03:42,537
But it grew far faster than anyone expected.

62
00:03:43,287 --> 00:03:47,494
Officers retreated into small secure rooms at the end of the corridors,

63
00:03:47,544 --> 00:03:49,172
as they had been trained to.

64
00:03:49,222 --> 00:03:52,709
But those rooms were not strong enough. Prisoners broke in.

65
00:03:52,759 --> 00:03:56,546
Officers were beaten, stripped of their keys, and taken hostage.

66
00:03:57,296 --> 00:03:58,506
Within about an hour,

67
00:03:58,556 --> 00:04:01,803
the whole of L block was in the hands of the prisoners.

68
00:04:01,853 --> 00:04:06,240
Hundreds of men. Eight cell blocks. And, according to the official report,

69
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:10,497
twelve correctional officers held hostage over the course of the siege.

70
00:04:11,247 --> 00:04:15,029
One man who was there later remembered how slowly the truth sank in.

71
00:04:15,079 --> 00:04:17,451
It took a long time to dawn on us, he said,

72
00:04:17,501 --> 00:04:19,198
that this is a full scale riot.

73
00:04:19,948 --> 00:04:22,657
And the warden himself, according to one account,

74
00:04:22,707 --> 00:04:25,529
was not told until around two hours after it began.

75
00:04:26,499 --> 00:04:28,517
Chapter 3. The first night.

76
00:04:30,647 --> 00:04:33,290
The first hours were the bloodiest of the entire siege.

77
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:37,912
Inside L block, some prisoners turned on other prisoners.

78
00:04:37,962 --> 00:04:39,953
Men suspected of being informants,

79
00:04:40,003 --> 00:04:44,035
snitches who had passed information to the guards, were hunted down.

80
00:04:44,785 --> 00:04:47,308
At least five prisoners were killed that first day,

81
00:04:47,358 --> 00:04:48,670
according to most accounts.

82
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:51,798
Their bodies were carried out and left in the recreation yard,

83
00:04:51,848 --> 00:04:53,665
where the authorities could see them.

84
00:04:54,415 --> 00:04:55,532
Rumors spread fast.

85
00:04:55,582 --> 00:04:59,589
One newspaper reported that nineteen bodies were piled in the gym.

86
00:04:59,639 --> 00:05:01,432
It was not true. But for days,

87
00:05:01,482 --> 00:05:04,690
nobody outside knew how many people were dead inside.

88
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:09,244
Families of the officers gathered outside the prison, waiting for news.

89
00:05:09,294 --> 00:05:11,415
Some of them would wait for eleven days.

90
00:05:11,465 --> 00:05:13,532
Every time a body appeared in the yard,

91
00:05:13,582 --> 00:05:15,215
they did not know whose it was.

92
00:05:15,965 --> 00:05:18,357
Three officers were released that night,

93
00:05:18,407 --> 00:05:21,960
injured and in need of medical care. Some accounts say two.

94
00:05:22,010 --> 00:05:23,487
The others stayed inside.

95
00:05:24,237 --> 00:05:25,027
Out on the yard,

96
00:05:25,077 --> 00:05:28,859
prisoners who had not been in the block were stuck between the two sides.

97
00:05:28,909 --> 00:05:31,641
At three twenty one in the morning, they surrendered.

98
00:05:31,691 --> 00:05:34,581
They were stripped and packed into cells, ten to a cell.

99
00:05:35,331 --> 00:05:37,794
By sunrise on Monday, the lines were drawn.

100
00:05:37,844 --> 00:05:41,125
The state controlled the walls, the towers, and the yard.

101
00:05:41,175 --> 00:05:44,631
The prisoners controlled L block. And they had the hostages.

102
00:05:45,601 --> 00:05:48,304
Chapter 4. Three gangs, one block.

103
00:05:50,434 --> 00:05:54,064
What made Lucasville different from most prison riots was who was in charge.

104
00:05:54,814 --> 00:05:59,034
Inside L block, there were three main groups. Muslim prisoners,

105
00:05:59,084 --> 00:06:03,982
who had started the protest. A black street gang, the Gangster Disciples.

106
00:06:04,032 --> 00:06:07,980
And a white supremacist prison gang, the Aryan Brotherhood.

107
00:06:08,730 --> 00:06:13,003
On the outside, these groups were enemies. Inside the occupied block,

108
00:06:13,053 --> 00:06:17,013
they made an alliance. One shout, reported from the first night,

109
00:06:17,063 --> 00:06:19,017
said it all. This is not racial.

110
00:06:19,767 --> 00:06:22,221
The groups split up the block between them.

111
00:06:22,271 --> 00:06:23,793
Each held its own hostages.

112
00:06:23,843 --> 00:06:27,578
The Aryan Brotherhood guarded the white officers known as racist.

113
00:06:27,628 --> 00:06:28,393
And every day,

114
00:06:28,443 --> 00:06:32,759
representatives of the three gangs met in an improvised leadership council.

115
00:06:33,509 --> 00:06:34,398
After the riot,

116
00:06:34,448 --> 00:06:38,465
investigators found two words written on a wall inside the block.

117
00:06:38,515 --> 00:06:43,033
Convict unity. Written, one reporter said, in what might have been paint.

118
00:06:43,083 --> 00:06:44,597
Or might have been blood.

119
00:06:45,947 --> 00:06:49,489
For the authorities, it was the worst possible situation.

120
00:06:49,539 --> 00:06:53,585
Not one gang to negotiate with. Three. Each with its own leaders,

121
00:06:53,635 --> 00:06:55,979
its own demands, and its own hostages.

122
00:06:56,729 --> 00:07:01,415
And the prisoners knew something important. The authorities had a choice. Talk,

123
00:07:01,465 --> 00:07:04,053
or storm the block. And in American history,

124
00:07:04,103 --> 00:07:07,051
storming a prison had already gone terribly wrong.

125
00:07:08,021 --> 00:07:10,074
Chapter 5. Why not storm it?

126
00:07:12,204 --> 00:07:14,273
In September nineteen seventy one,

127
00:07:14,323 --> 00:07:18,075
inmates took over Attica prison in New York. After four days,

128
00:07:18,125 --> 00:07:21,067
the state sent in troopers and guards with guns.

129
00:07:21,117 --> 00:07:26,053
When the shooting stopped, forty three people were dead, including ten hostages.

130
00:07:26,103 --> 00:07:29,294
Most of them were killed by the state's own gunfire.

131
00:07:30,044 --> 00:07:34,632
In February nineteen eighty, inmates seized the state penitentiary of New Mexico.

132
00:07:34,682 --> 00:07:36,923
They held it for about thirty six hours.

133
00:07:36,973 --> 00:07:41,333
Thirty three prisoners were killed by other prisoners, many of them tortured.

134
00:07:41,383 --> 00:07:43,108
Officers were beaten and raped.

135
00:07:43,158 --> 00:07:47,403
It became known as one of the most brutal prison riots in American history.

136
00:07:48,153 --> 00:07:51,976
Ohio's leaders had both of those stories in mind. Storm L block,

137
00:07:52,026 --> 00:07:54,638
and the hostages might die in the crossfire,

138
00:07:54,688 --> 00:07:57,664
or be killed before the troops reached them. Wait,

139
00:07:57,714 --> 00:08:00,145
and the prisoners might kill them anyway.

140
00:08:00,895 --> 00:08:04,918
L block was also hard to take. Long corridors. Barricaded doors.

141
00:08:04,968 --> 00:08:07,972
Hundreds of prisoners who knew every inch of it.

142
00:08:08,022 --> 00:08:11,600
Any assault team would have had to fight its way through,

143
00:08:11,650 --> 00:08:12,809
one gate at a time.

144
00:08:13,559 --> 00:08:17,390
So the state chose to wait, to negotiate, and to squeeze.

145
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:21,815
Cut the water. Cut the power. Control the food. And keep talking.

146
00:08:22,785 --> 00:08:25,105
Chapter 6. Days two to four.

147
00:08:27,235 --> 00:08:31,334
On Monday morning, day two, the prisoners broke off phone contact.

148
00:08:31,384 --> 00:08:35,419
They wanted one thing before any hostage was released. The media.

149
00:08:36,169 --> 00:08:39,566
They wanted local and national news to hear their side of the story.

150
00:08:39,616 --> 00:08:41,949
The state did not want to give them a platform.

151
00:08:42,699 --> 00:08:45,841
The authorities cut the water and electricity to L block.

152
00:08:45,891 --> 00:08:49,761
Prisoners set up a loudspeaker to shout their demands across the yard.

153
00:08:49,811 --> 00:08:51,161
According to one account,

154
00:08:51,211 --> 00:08:54,296
officials drowned it out with the noise of a helicopter.

155
00:08:55,046 --> 00:08:58,460
Two prisoners walked out into the yard carrying a white flag,

156
00:08:58,510 --> 00:09:01,810
to ask again for reporters. One of them was George Skatzes,

157
00:09:01,860 --> 00:09:03,741
a member of the Aryan Brotherhood,

158
00:09:03,791 --> 00:09:05,841
who became one of the main spokesmen.

159
00:09:06,591 --> 00:09:10,030
Outside the walls, Ohio was assembling a small army.

160
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:13,653
State Highway Patrol troopers. SWAT teams. FBI agents.

161
00:09:13,703 --> 00:09:15,532
And the Ohio National Guard,

162
00:09:15,582 --> 00:09:19,557
whose soldiers ended up sleeping in the prison's farm barns,

163
00:09:19,607 --> 00:09:21,570
next to the cows and the pigs.

164
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:23,038
On day three,

165
00:09:23,088 --> 00:09:27,642
a State Highway Patrol helicopter crashed while landing on the prison grounds.

166
00:09:27,692 --> 00:09:32,542
Accounts differ on whether anyone was hurt. Refrigerated trucks were parked nearby,

167
00:09:32,592 --> 00:09:34,254
to be used as mobile morgues.

168
00:09:35,004 --> 00:09:39,090
Journalists camped in a corner of the prison property. It rained.

169
00:09:39,140 --> 00:09:42,335
The media area turned into a pit of mud. And still,

170
00:09:42,385 --> 00:09:45,071
for days, very little information came out.

171
00:09:45,821 --> 00:09:50,434
Inside, conditions got worse every day. No running water. No electricity.

172
00:09:50,484 --> 00:09:54,651
Toilets that could not flush. Hundreds of men, and their hostages,

173
00:09:54,701 --> 00:09:57,270
living in the dark in a wrecked building.

174
00:09:58,020 --> 00:09:59,924
On day four, a bedsheet appeared,

175
00:09:59,974 --> 00:10:01,878
hanging from a window of L block.

176
00:10:01,928 --> 00:10:04,543
The message on it threatened to kill a guard.

177
00:10:05,293 --> 00:10:10,004
A prison spokeswoman told reporters it was a standard threat. Nothing new.

178
00:10:11,554 --> 00:10:14,585
Inside L block, that sentence landed very differently.

179
00:10:14,635 --> 00:10:18,066
According to later testimony and accounts by former hostages,

180
00:10:18,116 --> 00:10:21,888
the prisoners took it as proof that the state did not believe them.

181
00:10:21,938 --> 00:10:23,999
And some of them decided to prove it.

182
00:10:24,969 --> 00:10:26,813
Chapter 7. Day five.

183
00:10:28,943 --> 00:10:32,040
On Thursday, April fifteenth, according to most accounts,

184
00:10:32,090 --> 00:10:34,083
the prisoners carried out the threat.

185
00:10:34,833 --> 00:10:38,962
Corrections officer Robert Vallandingham was one of the hostages.

186
00:10:39,712 --> 00:10:41,647
He was strangled inside L block.

187
00:10:41,697 --> 00:10:43,569
His body was taken to a window,

188
00:10:43,619 --> 00:10:46,049
and pushed out into the recreation yard.

189
00:10:47,599 --> 00:10:50,393
Exactly who killed him has never been fully settled.

190
00:10:50,443 --> 00:10:53,182
The prosecution later said the killing was planned.

191
00:10:53,232 --> 00:10:55,425
Different witnesses blamed different men.

192
00:10:55,475 --> 00:10:59,964
One prosecutor admitted the state did not know for certain whose hands had done it.

193
00:11:00,714 --> 00:11:05,323
Outside, the pressure to storm the block was enormous. But Ohio's governor,

194
00:11:05,373 --> 00:11:07,001
George Voinovich, resisted.

195
00:11:07,051 --> 00:11:10,666
An assault on a block full of armed prisoners and hostages,

196
00:11:10,716 --> 00:11:13,461
the state feared, could turn into a massacre.

197
00:11:14,211 --> 00:11:16,353
Ohio's lieutenant governor at the time,

198
00:11:16,403 --> 00:11:19,612
the man in charge of the crisis for the governor's office,

199
00:11:19,662 --> 00:11:23,265
was Mike DeWine. Decades later, he would become governor himself.

200
00:11:24,015 --> 00:11:28,111
The state kept talking. But now, everybody knew the threats were real.

201
00:11:29,681 --> 00:11:31,153
Chapter 8. On the air.

202
00:11:33,283 --> 00:11:37,633
After Vallandingham's death, the negotiations finally began to move.

203
00:11:38,383 --> 00:11:43,675
Two hostages were released. Officers Darrold Clark and Anthony Demons. In exchange,

204
00:11:43,725 --> 00:11:47,730
the prisoners got what they had wanted from the start. A voice.

205
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:52,345
George Skatzes was allowed to speak on the radio. The next morning,

206
00:11:52,395 --> 00:11:56,844
a Muslim prisoner, Stanley Cummings, spoke on television. For the first time,

207
00:11:56,894 --> 00:11:59,590
the outside world heard the prisoners directly.

208
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:02,930
The message was not what many people expected.

209
00:12:02,980 --> 00:12:06,889
The prisoners talked about overcrowding. About the tuberculosis test.

210
00:12:06,939 --> 00:12:09,759
About treatment by staff. And about the fear that,

211
00:12:09,809 --> 00:12:13,087
once they came out, they would be punished for everything,

212
00:12:13,137 --> 00:12:14,924
whether they had done it or not.

213
00:12:15,674 --> 00:12:18,312
On day six, food and water were sent in.

214
00:12:18,362 --> 00:12:20,327
It was seen as a breakthrough.

215
00:12:21,077 --> 00:12:24,372
And while the talking went on, the state was also listening.

216
00:12:24,422 --> 00:12:26,824
According to the governor's press secretary,

217
00:12:26,874 --> 00:12:31,450
the FBI drilled listening devices into the floor of the room used for negotiations.

218
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:35,519
Other accounts say microphones were dropped in through holes in the roof.

219
00:12:36,269 --> 00:12:40,509
The prisoners had their own rules. According to the state's press secretary,

220
00:12:40,559 --> 00:12:44,742
they kept a schedule, and stopped negotiating every evening at six o'clock.

221
00:12:45,492 --> 00:12:48,265
Darrold Clark, one of the released hostages,

222
00:12:48,315 --> 00:12:52,178
said years later what he had felt inside. I thought, he said,

223
00:12:52,228 --> 00:12:53,397
I was going to die.

224
00:12:54,367 --> 00:12:56,675
Chapter 9. Twenty one points.

225
00:12:58,805 --> 00:13:01,528
The way out came through a lawyer. Niki Schwartz,

226
00:13:01,578 --> 00:13:03,735
a civil rights attorney from Cleveland,

227
00:13:03,785 --> 00:13:06,395
was brought in to help the prisoners negotiate.

228
00:13:07,145 --> 00:13:12,261
He later said he told the prisoners that an agreement made under duress would not legally bind the state.

229
00:13:12,311 --> 00:13:15,607
But he would only take part if the authorities promised to honor it.

230
00:13:16,357 --> 00:13:19,222
The result was a document with twenty one points.

231
00:13:19,272 --> 00:13:23,804
The prisoners would surrender, and release the remaining hostages. In return,

232
00:13:23,854 --> 00:13:25,946
the state made a series of promises.

233
00:13:26,696 --> 00:13:30,743
No retaliation against any prisoner or group of prisoners.

234
00:13:30,793 --> 00:13:35,828
Fair and unbiased discipline and prosecutions. A review of medical care,

235
00:13:35,878 --> 00:13:40,984
and of the tuberculosis testing. Better food. Changes to mail and visits.

236
00:13:41,034 --> 00:13:42,326
Outside monitoring.

237
00:13:43,076 --> 00:13:46,982
Other points dealt with daily life. Lower prices in the prison shop.

238
00:13:47,032 --> 00:13:51,345
Better jobs. Prisoners allowed to have an adviser at disciplinary hearings.

239
00:13:51,395 --> 00:13:56,522
And a promise that the state would not force prisoners of different races to share cells.

240
00:13:57,272 --> 00:13:59,813
The warden signed the document. For the prisoners,

241
00:13:59,863 --> 00:14:02,507
those twenty one points were their insurance policy.

242
00:14:02,557 --> 00:14:07,326
Whether the state kept its word would become one of the longest arguments of the whole story.

243
00:14:08,076 --> 00:14:12,498
On Wednesday, April twenty first, the eleventh day, the surrender began.

244
00:14:12,548 --> 00:14:17,095
It was broadcast live, for hours, on a Cincinnati area television station.

245
00:14:17,845 --> 00:14:19,779
The remaining hostages walked out.

246
00:14:19,829 --> 00:14:23,689
Then the prisoners came out into the yard, in groups. Mostly naked,

247
00:14:23,739 --> 00:14:24,856
with their hands up.

248
00:14:26,206 --> 00:14:31,304
When officials finally walked into L block, they found devastation. Cells gutted.

249
00:14:31,354 --> 00:14:36,261
Walls smashed. Ohio's lieutenant governor called it exactly that. Devastation.

250
00:14:36,311 --> 00:14:40,264
The official damage estimate was more than ten million dollars.

251
00:14:40,314 --> 00:14:42,616
Some later estimates were far higher.

252
00:14:43,366 --> 00:14:45,754
Nine prisoners and one officer were dead.

253
00:14:45,804 --> 00:14:49,500
It was the longest and deadliest prison riot in Ohio's history,

254
00:14:49,550 --> 00:14:51,641
according to the state's own report.

255
00:14:52,611 --> 00:14:55,082
Chapter 10. The Lucasville Five.

256
00:14:57,212 --> 00:15:01,204
The surrender was only the beginning of a second battle. In court.

257
00:15:01,954 --> 00:15:05,767
Around fifty prisoners were charged with crimes connected to the riot.

258
00:15:05,817 --> 00:15:08,581
According to one count, forty seven were convicted.

259
00:15:09,331 --> 00:15:11,463
Five men were sentenced to death.

260
00:15:11,513 --> 00:15:15,827
They became known as the Lucasville Five. Siddique Abdullah Hasan,

261
00:15:15,877 --> 00:15:20,323
the Muslim prisoner the state said had planned and led the uprising.

262
00:15:20,373 --> 00:15:24,952
Jason Robb and George Skatzes, from the Aryan Brotherhood. James Were,

263
00:15:25,002 --> 00:15:28,258
also known as Namir Abdul Mateen. And Keith LaMar.

264
00:15:29,008 --> 00:15:31,833
Much of the evidence came from other prisoners,

265
00:15:31,883 --> 00:15:34,586
some of whom had made deals with prosecutors.

266
00:15:34,636 --> 00:15:38,012
One of the most important witnesses was Anthony Lavelle,

267
00:15:38,062 --> 00:15:41,193
the leader of the Gangster Disciples inside L block.

268
00:15:41,243 --> 00:15:43,334
He received a far lighter sentence.

269
00:15:44,084 --> 00:15:49,665
Supporters of the five argue they were convicted on the testimony of informants with every reason to lie,

270
00:15:49,715 --> 00:15:53,848
and that the promise of fair prosecutions in the twenty one points was broken.

271
00:15:53,898 --> 00:15:56,154
Keith LaMar has always said he is innocent.

272
00:15:56,204 --> 00:15:58,085
The state stands by the convictions.

273
00:15:58,835 --> 00:16:02,194
In January twenty eleven, three of the five, Keith LaMar,

274
00:16:02,244 --> 00:16:04,527
Siddique Abdullah Hasan and Jason Robb,

275
00:16:04,577 --> 00:16:07,637
went on hunger strike at the supermax in Youngstown.

276
00:16:07,687 --> 00:16:10,209
They demanded more time out of their cells,

277
00:16:10,259 --> 00:16:13,857
physical contact with their families, and longer phone calls.

278
00:16:13,907 --> 00:16:17,745
After about twelve days, the state agreed to change its policies,

279
00:16:17,795 --> 00:16:19,001
and the strike ended.

280
00:16:19,751 --> 00:16:21,787
The case has been revisited in books,

281
00:16:21,837 --> 00:16:24,211
documentaries and court filings ever since.

282
00:16:24,261 --> 00:16:27,312
The arguments are the same as in nineteen ninety three.

283
00:16:27,362 --> 00:16:30,864
Who really planned the killings. Who just happened to be there.

284
00:16:30,914 --> 00:16:34,811
And whether the state kept the promise it made to get its prison back.

285
00:16:35,561 --> 00:16:39,035
Decades later, all five are still on Ohio's death row.

286
00:16:39,085 --> 00:16:42,952
Ohio has not carried out an execution since twenty eighteen,

287
00:16:43,002 --> 00:16:47,978
and the governor has issued repeated reprieves. Keith LaMar's execution date,

288
00:16:48,028 --> 00:16:50,328
as of now, is set for twenty thirty.

289
00:16:51,078 --> 00:16:53,998
The governor issuing those reprieves is Mike DeWine.

290
00:16:54,048 --> 00:16:57,083
The same man who was in charge of the crisis response,

291
00:16:57,133 --> 00:16:58,796
back in nineteen ninety three.

292
00:16:59,766 --> 00:17:01,703
Chapter 11. What changed.

293
00:17:03,833 --> 00:17:06,186
Lucasville changed the Ohio prison system.

294
00:17:06,936 --> 00:17:12,484
The state paid four point one million dollars to settle claims brought on behalf of inmate victims,

295
00:17:12,534 --> 00:17:15,594
and agreed to deal with overcrowding and mismanagement.

296
00:17:16,344 --> 00:17:19,427
Guard posts were rebuilt with enclosed escape routes,

297
00:17:19,477 --> 00:17:23,093
so officers could not be trapped the way they were in L block.

298
00:17:23,143 --> 00:17:26,344
Double bunking of maximum security prisoners was ended.

299
00:17:26,394 --> 00:17:29,241
Classification and movement rules were rewritten.

300
00:17:29,991 --> 00:17:33,265
And Ohio built something new. In May nineteen ninety eight,

301
00:17:33,315 --> 00:17:37,208
the Ohio State Penitentiary opened near Youngstown. A supermax prison,

302
00:17:37,258 --> 00:17:41,602
where the most dangerous prisoners spend most of the day alone in their cells.

303
00:17:41,652 --> 00:17:45,039
Several of the men convicted over Lucasville were sent there.

304
00:17:45,789 --> 00:17:51,392
The communication failures during the siege also pushed the state to build a new statewide radio system,

305
00:17:51,442 --> 00:17:52,208
so that police,

306
00:17:52,258 --> 00:17:56,013
prison staff and the National Guard could actually talk to each other.

307
00:17:56,763 --> 00:18:01,931
One former member of the SWAT team that stood outside L block in nineteen ninety three said,

308
00:18:01,981 --> 00:18:04,710
thirty years later, that the prison is safer now.

309
00:18:04,760 --> 00:18:08,963
The current warden says the changes made after the riot are still in place.

310
00:18:09,713 --> 00:18:13,553
Ohio's former prison director later called the riot a wake up call.

311
00:18:13,603 --> 00:18:15,876
Not just for the prison system, he said,

312
00:18:15,926 --> 00:18:17,269
but for the whole state.

313
00:18:18,019 --> 00:18:22,871
Every year, officers remember Robert Vallandingham. On the thirtieth anniversary,

314
00:18:22,921 --> 00:18:26,260
in twenty twenty three, a wreath was laid in his memory.

315
00:18:27,010 --> 00:18:30,773
Today, the Southern Ohio Correctional Facility is still open.

316
00:18:30,823 --> 00:18:34,461
It still holds some of the most dangerous men in the state.

317
00:18:34,511 --> 00:18:38,087
L block was rebuilt. And from the hills around Lucasville,

318
00:18:38,137 --> 00:18:42,150
it looks almost exactly the same as it did on that Easter Sunday.

319
00:18:42,900 --> 00:18:47,364
And the questions that started it are still with us. Overcrowded prisons.

320
00:18:47,414 --> 00:18:51,322
Gangs. Officers outnumbered. And a system that, for eleven days,

321
00:18:51,372 --> 00:18:53,362
lost control of its own building.

322
00:18:54,912 --> 00:18:58,377
So what do you think? Could the state have ended it sooner,

323
00:18:58,427 --> 00:19:01,653
or did negotiating save lives? Tell us in the comments.

324
00:19:01,703 --> 00:19:06,120
And if you want to see a prison where the inmates never gave the keys back,

325
00:19:06,170 --> 00:19:08,740
watch our story about Tocoron, in Venezuela.
